#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158576 :tutorial/content "The **associative law for conjunctions** states that when we have multiple conjunctions in a row, the placement of parentheses does not matter:\n\n$$\n(A \\land B) \\land C \\equiv A \\land (B \\land C)\n$$\n\nWe can prove this equivalence using a truth table.\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,C\\,$ | $\\, A \\land B\\,$ | $\\, B \\land C\\,$ | $\\, (A \\land B) \\land C\\,$ | $\\, A\\land (B \\land C)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n\nIn natural language, the associative law for conjunctions amounts to moving a comma between \"and\" statements. For example, the associative law states that the sentence\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{The cat is quiet}}_{(A} \\quad \\underbrace{\\textrm{and}\\vphantom{p}}_{\\land} \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{the dog is loud}}_{B)} \\quad , \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{and}}_{\\land} \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{the turtle is slow}}_{C}\n$$\n\nis logically equivalent to the sentence\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{The cat is quiet}}_{A} \\quad ,  \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{and}}_{\\land} \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{the dog is loud}}_{(B} \\quad \\underbrace{\\textrm{and}\\vphantom{p}}_{\\land} \\quad \\underbrace{\\textrm{the turtle is slow}}_{C)}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ce01eb47-13d6-5f82-8e3e-f1bd91846d71" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7974 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158577 :tutorial/content "The **commutative law for conjunctions** states that we can switch the order of a conjunction:\n\n$$\nA \\land B \\equiv B \\land A\n$$\n\nWe can prove this result using a truth table.\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,A \\land B\\,$ | $\\,B \\land A\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n\nIn natural language, the commutative law for conjunctions amounts to swapping two phrases surrounding the word \"and.\" For example, the commutative law states that the sentence\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{The cat is quiet}}_{(A} \\quad \\underbrace{\\textrm{and}\\vphantom{p}}_{\\land} \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{the dog is loud}}_{B)}\n$$\n\nis logically equivalent to the sentence\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{The dog is loud}}_{(B} \\quad \\underbrace{\\textrm{and}\\vphantom{p}}_{\\land} \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{the cat is quiet}}_{A)}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e31a6885-bdfb-5354-8825-aecfd2a54a20" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7975 :tutorial/title "The Commutative Law for Conjunctions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158586 :tutorial/content "The **associative law for disjunctions** is similar:\n\n$$\n(A \\lor B) \\lor C \\equiv A \\lor (B \\lor C)\n$$\n\nWe can prove this equivalence using a truth table.\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,C\\,$ | $\\, A \\lor B\\,$ | $\\, B \\lor C\\,$ | $\\, (A \\lor B) \\lor C\\,$ | $\\, A\\lor (B \\lor C)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n\nIn natural language, the associative law for disjunctions amounts to moving a comma between \"or\" statements. For example, the associative law states that the sentence\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{The cat is quiet}}_{(A} \\quad \\underbrace{\\textrm{or}\\vphantom{p}}_{\\lor} \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{the dog is loud}}_{B)} \\quad , \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{or}}_{\\lor} \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{the turtle is slow}}_{C}\n$$\n\nis logically equivalent to the sentence\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{The cat is quiet}}_{A} \\quad ,  \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{or}}_{\\lor} \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{the dog is loud}}_{(B} \\quad \\underbrace{\\textrm{or}\\vphantom{p}}_{\\lor} \\quad \\underbrace{\\textrm{the turtle is slow}}_{C)}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c6260eed-7f32-50cb-8351-8a4f61b4f3fe" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9053 :tutorial/title "The Associative Law for Disjunctions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158587 :tutorial/content "The **commutative law for disjunctions** is similar:\n\n$$\nA \\lor B \\equiv B \\lor A\n$$\n\nWe can prove this result using a truth table.\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,A \\lor B\\,$ | $\\,B \\lor A\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n\nLikewise, in natural language, the commutative law for disjunctions amounts to swapping two phrases surrounding the word \"or.\" For example, the commutative law states that the sentence\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{The cat is quiet}}_{(A} \\quad \\underbrace{\\textrm{or}\\vphantom{p}}_{\\lor} \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{the dog is loud}}_{B)}\n$$\n\nis logically equivalent to the sentence\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{The dog is loud}}_{(B} \\quad \\underbrace{\\textrm{or}\\vphantom{p}}_{\\lor} \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{the cat is quiet}}_{A)}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "995e4380-991c-589d-9001-fc91cbd2d0b2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9054 :tutorial/title "The Commutative Law for Disjunctions"}]}
